<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective memory management is essential for large language model (LLM) agents handling long-term interactions.
Current memory frameworks typically treat agents as passive “recorders” and retrieve information without understanding its deeper implications.
They may fail in scenarios requiring conflict detection and complex decision-making.
To bridge this critical gap, we propose a novel actionable memory framework called ActMem that integrates memory retrieval with active causal reasoning.
ActMem transforms unstructured dialogue history into a structured causal and semantic graph.
By leveraging counterfactual reasoning and commonsense completion, it enables agents to deduce implicit constraints and resolve potential conflicts between past states and current intentions.
Furthermore, we introduce a comprehensive dataset ActMemEval to evaluate agent reasoning capabilities in logic-driven scenarios,
moving beyond the fact-retrieval focus of existing memory benchmarks.
Experiments demonstrate that ActMem significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in handling complex, memory-dependent tasks, paving the way for more consistent and reliable intelligent assistants.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in natural language understanding and generation, serving as the backbone for various intelligent agents.
To enable these agents to handle long-term interactions, effective memory management is essential <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025c)</a></cite>.
Conventionally, memory frameworks in LLM agents focus on expanding context windows or utilizing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to store, update and recall historical interactions.
These methods aim to improve <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the capacity of memory recall</span>, ensuring that the agent can retrieve specific information from a vast repository of past dialogues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, a fundamental gap remains between simply remembering the past and effectively using it.
Existing memory management methods typically serve as a passive “recorder,”
where the goal is to compress, summarize, and retrieve historical text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2026)</a></cite>.
While RAG-based methods can fetch relevant dialogue snippets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite>, they often fail to understand the deeper implications of that memory for current decision-making.
In real-world applications, an agent acts as a human assistant, not just a “archivist” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
For instance, when a user proposes a new schedule, the agent should not only recall previous similar ones but also find event conflicts and offer adjustment suggestions.
This requires the agent to go beyond memory retrieval and perform active reasoning based on historical states.
We define this capability as <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the utility of memory for action</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This limitation is also reflected in current memory management benchmarks.
Existing datasets, such as LoCoMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al. (2024)</a></cite>, LongMemEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and HaluMem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, primarily evaluate the accuracy of memory retrieval through QA tasks.
They assess whether the agent can “find” the answer but overlook whether the agent can “reason” upon it.
They lack scenarios involving complex causal reasoning where the answer is not explicitly stated in the history but must be deduced.
Their evaluations fail to measure how well an agent can utilize memory to assist in current planning that may conflict with past state.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.00026v1/example.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>A motivating comparison between memory retrieval and our proposed memory reasoning setting.
(a) An example from LongMemEval representing memory retrieval.
(b) An example illustrating the necessity of memory reasoning. Although the user’s current query (buying Sago Palms) does not semantically overlap with the past memory (a teething puppy), the agent should infer the latent conflict based on the common sense that Sago Palms are toxic.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To clearly illustrate the limitations of current benchmarks and the motivation behind our work,
we present a comparison in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Subfigure (a) shows a typical example from LongMemEval.
The user’s query explicitly matches a record in the past memory summaries.
The agent only needs to perform semantic or vectorized matching to retrieve the fact.
This is a task that evaluates the capacity of memory recall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Subfigure (b) shows a more realistic and challenging scenario that necessitates memory reasoning.
The user asks for the location to buy “Sago Palms.”
A retrieval-based agent would simply provide purchasing information (as indicated by the incorrect response).
However, the dialogue history contains a critical information:
the user has a puppy that is “teething” and “chewing on everything.”
Given that Sago Palms are highly toxic to dogs,
a responsible agent should infer the potential danger and intervene with a warning.
We find that LLMs generally possess this commonsense knowledge,
but under existing agent memory management frameworks, they tend to overlook it and thus struggle to leverage it for auxiliary reasoning.
The inability to reason over memory can lead to conflicting advice or even dangerous recommendations.
This example highlights the gap we aim to bridge: transforming the agent from a passive retriever to an active reasoner that ensures the utility of memory for action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge this gap, we propose ActMem, a new memory management framework that integrates memory retrieval with causal reasoning.
It first extracts memory facts to summarize raw dialogue history.
Then, it utilizes embedding-based clustering to group topic-related facts and establish semantic edges between fact pairs that exceed a similarity threshold to preserve contextual connectivity.
After that, it mines causal relations of facts.
Furthermore, it performs counterfactual reasoning, commonsense completion and PMI-based filtering to refine cause edges.
The facts with their semantic and causal edges form a memory knowledge graph (KG).
This allows the agent to identify associations, resolve mutual exclusions, and deduce implicit constraints that influence current actions.
Finally, to evaluate this new setting, we introduce a new dataset called ActMemEval.
Unlike traditional retrieval-based benchmarks,
our dataset focuses on scenarios requiring active reasoning and causal deduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify the critical limitation of current agent memory systems: the disconnect between memory retrieval and reasoning. We propose a shift in focus from the “capacity of recall” to the “utility of memory for action.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce ActMem, an actionable memory management framework. By integrating a memory KG with causal and semantic edges, ActMem enables agents to utilize past experiences for robust decision-making.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a comprehensive dataset ActMemEval to evaluate the reasoning capabilities of agents in long-term interactions, covering complex tasks that require logic-driven memory utilization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extensive experiments demonstrate that ActMem significantly outperforms existing baselines in handling complex, logic-driven tasks. It also achieves competitive and even SOTA performance on conventional datasets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We hope this work will pave the way for future research into more consistent and human-aligned intelligent assistants.
Our source code and dataset is available at GitHub. <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          <a href="https://github.com/nju-websoft/ActMem" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/nju-websoft/ActMem</a></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Agent Memory Management</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Memory management is recognized as a core capability for intelligent agents, enabling continuity in long‑term interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Existing studies can be grouped into three paradigms: system‑level management, structured organization, and experience‑based guidance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OS‑style memory management.</span>
Early work seeks to address the context‑window limitations of LLMs.
MemGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al. (2023)</a></cite> introduces OS‑inspired mechanisms such as paging and segmentation, inspiring later frameworks like MemOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> and MemoryOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
In parallel, retrieval‑centric systems such as Mem0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chhikara et al. (2025)</a></cite> and MemoryBank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al. (2024)</a></cite> adopted a RAG‑style paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite>, emphasizing memory lifecycle management, i.e, storage, retrieval, and updating, to ensure efficient access to relevant history.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured memory management.</span>
To overcome the limitations of flat text, recent work explores structured formats for better memory organization.
Examples include tree‑based designs like MemTree <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rezazadeh et al. (2025)</a></cite> and graph‑based framework such as A‑Mem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Other studies propose hierarchical or compositional memory frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasmussen et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> to capture dependencies across dialogue turns.
These methods show that introducing topology significantly improves retrieval precision over unstructured text chunks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory as execution guidance.</span>
Beyond fact recall,
another line of research leverages memory to guide decision‑making through past experiences. This includes trajectory‑level retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, where agents imitate successful workflows, and case‑based reuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a></cite>, where subtasks are retrieved to support intermediate reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite>
These methods aim to extract procedural patterns or heuristics from execution histories to support planning in new scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussions.</span>
Despite these advances, a key gap remains. As highlighted in recent surveys <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, existing methods largely treat memory as passive storage with fixed representations.
Whether using RAG or trajectory replay, retrieval still relies heavily on surface‑level semantic similarity, making it difficult to detect implicit conflicts or adapt past experiences to dynamic environments.
This often leads to negative transfer or hallucinations.
In contrast, our proposed ActMem adopts an event‑centric design that explicitly encodes logical structures and causal dependencies, transforming memory from passive storage into an active reasoning engine.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.00026v1/framework.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/164;" width="429" height="164" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The overview of our proposed framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Synthetic Evaluation Benchmark</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Synthetic evaluation benchmarks have emerged as a popular paradigm for assessing the capabilities of LLMs in controlled manner.
Currently, synthetic evaluation has evolved from assessing simple information retention to measuring complex reasoning capabilities.
Recent initiatives, including RULER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al. (2024)</a></cite> and BABILong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuratov et al. (2024)</a></cite> include variable tracking and logical chaining, while other datasets like LoCoMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maharana et al. (2024)</a></cite> and LongMemEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite> target long-term dialogue consistency and agent memory retrieval.
Despite these advancements, existing benchmarks remain QA-oriented, assessing whether an agent can passively retrieve information rather than actively utilize it for decision-making.
We address this limitation with a new dataset ActMemEval, which specifically evaluates the utility of memory for action through scenarios involving implicit constraints and causal conflicts.
We ensure the reliability of the dataset through human verification.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The ActMem Framework</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present ActMem to bridge the gap between passive retrieval and active reasoning in LLM agents.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Agent Memory Management ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the framework consists of four modules:
(1) memory fact extraction,
(2) fact clustering,
(3) memory KG construction,
and
(4) counterfactual-based retrieval and reasoning.
Formally, we define the raw dialogue history as <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{u_{1},a_{1},u_{2},a_{2},\dots,u_{t},a_{t},\dots,u_{N},a_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{u_{1},a_{1},u_{2},a_{2},\dots,u_{t},a_{t},\dots,u_{N},a_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the user and agent utterances at turn <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Our goal is to transform <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> into a structured memory graph <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to support complex decision-making.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Memory Fact Extraction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Raw dialogue history often contains redundancy and noise that hinder effective reasoning.
To address this, we first compress the raw history <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> into a set of atomic memory facts <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We employ an LLM-based information extractor to parse the dialogue and generate declarative sentences containing key entities and facts following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
We define a extraction function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{ext}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>ext</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{ext}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> powered by an LLM to map raw dialogues into a set of atomic facts <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each dialogue turn <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract a subset of facts:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{ext}},u_{t},a_{t})=\{f_{t,1},f_{t,2},\dots,f_{t,k}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>ext</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{ext}},u_{t},a_{t})=\{f_{t,1},f_{t,2},\dots,f_{t,k}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{ext}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>ext</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{ext}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the extraction prompt designed to identify entities and events. The global fact set is the union of all turns: <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\bigcup_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\bigcup_{t=1}^{N}\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unlike traditional summarization which compresses the entire context into a single paragraph, our method focuses on <span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">atomicity</span>.
This granular extraction serves as the foundation for subsequent causal mining.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Fact Clustering</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To model long-term interactions effectively, it is essential to identify relationships between facts spanning different sessions.
Besides, mining causal edges by computing relations across all pairs of facts in the global history is time-consuming.
To mitigate this, we introduce a fact clustering module.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> be an embedding function, e.g., Qwen3-Embedding-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>,
that maps a textual fact <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute the semantic similarity between two facts using cosine similarity:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{i}=\phi(f_{i}),\ \ \ \text{sim}(f_{i},f_{j})=\frac{\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{h}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{h}_{i}\|\|\mathbf{h}_{j}\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{i}=\phi(f_{i}),\ \ \ \text{sim}(f_{i},f_{j})=\frac{\mathbf{h}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{h}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{h}_{i}\|\|\mathbf{h}_{j}\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To partition <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> into disjoint clusters <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{C_{1},\dots,C_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{C_{1},\dots,C_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently, we employ a single-pass incremental clustering algorithm.
We maintain a centroid vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for each cluster. For each incoming fact <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we calculate its similarity with existing centroids. If the maximum similarity exceeds a threshold <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the fact is assigned to the corresponding cluster; otherwise, a new cluster is initialized with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as its centroid.
This strategy ensures that facts related to the same topic (e.g., “puppy care” or “plant shopping”) are consolidated dynamically.
It allows us to perform causal mining within local clusters, significantly reducing computational overhead while preserving topical coherence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Memory KG Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core of ActMem is the construction of the memory KG, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math>, where nodes <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to memory facts and edges <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> represent causal or semantic relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantics edge generation.</span>
Following clustering, for each fact cluster, we calculate the pairwise similarity between facts and add a semantic edge between any two nodes whose similarity exceeds a predefined threshold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Raw causal edge generation.</span>
Within each cluster <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and each dialogue session,
we prompt the LLM to identify potential causal edges.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of candidate pairs <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{i},f_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{i},f_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> identified by the model as potentially causal (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\to f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\to f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{cause}},C_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>cause</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{cause}},C_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PMI-based hallucination filtering.</span> A major challenge in using LLMs for causal mining is “causal hallucination” where the model identifies spurious correlations as causation.
To verify the candidate edges in <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a verification mechanism based on Pointwise Mutual Information (PMI).
It quantifies the information gain provided by the antecedent fact <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for generating the consequent fact <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the verification score <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{PMI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>PMI</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{PMI}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the reduction in the negative log-likelihood of generating <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> when conditioned on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{PMI}}(f_{i},f_{j})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(f_{j}\mid T_{\text{uncond}})-\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(f_{j}\mid T_{\text{cond}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>PMI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>uncond</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>cond</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{PMI}}(f_{i},f_{j})=\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(f_{j}\mid T_{\text{uncond}})-\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(f_{j}\mid T_{\text{cond}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{M}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cross-entropy loss of the target sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> computed by a pre-trained language model <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., GPT2-Large) .
To model the causal dependency, we design specific prompt templates for the input contexts:
(1) The <span id="S3.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional context</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{cond}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>cond</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{cond}}</annotation></semantics></math> constructs the sequence “<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\text{. {As a result}, }f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>. </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">As a result</mtext><mtext>, </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\text{. {As a result}, }f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”;
(2) The <span id="S3.SS3.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unconditional context</span> <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{uncond}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>uncond</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{uncond}}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a neutral prefix “<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{{The fact is that} }f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">The fact is that</mtext><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{{The fact is that} }f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”.
A positive <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{PMI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>PMI</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{PMI}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the presence of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> significantly lowers the uncertainty of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, we retain only the pairs with a PMI score exceeding a threshold <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{causal}=\{(f_{i},f_{j})\in\bigcup\mathcal{H}_{k}\mid S_{\text{PMI}}(f_{i},f_{j})&gt;\tau\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>PMI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{causal}=\{(f_{i},f_{j})\in\bigcup\mathcal{H}_{k}\mid S_{\text{PMI}}(f_{i},f_{j})&gt;\tau\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, we merge these validated causal edges with semantic edges to form the final memory graph <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Counterfactual-based Retrieval and Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional RAG systems retrieve memories solely based on the semantic similarity to the user’s current query <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, often missing implicit constraints.
We propose a retrieval-reasoning-refinement loop to utilize the memory KG effectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Initial retrieval.</span> We retrieve a subset of facts <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{init}\subset\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{init}\subset\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> based on vector similarity with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Counterfactual reasoning.</span> To bridge the gap between the query and implicit memory constraints, we generate commonsense knowledge by asking a counterfactual question:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{cs}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{counter}},q,\mathcal{V}_{init})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>counter</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{cs}=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{counter}},q,\mathcal{V}_{init}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The question is “If the user does <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, what negative consequences might occur considering <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{init}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{init}</annotation></semantics></math> or general knowledge?”</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Refinement and response.</span> We use <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{cs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{cs}</annotation></semantics></math> as an enhanced query to perform a secondary retrieval, obtaining <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{refine}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{refine}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final context is <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{final}=\mathcal{V}_{init}\cup\{k_{cs}\}\cup\mathcal{V}_{refine}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{final}=\mathcal{V}_{init}\cup\{k_{cs}\}\cup\mathcal{V}_{refine}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The agent generates the final response <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ans" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ans</annotation></semantics></math> given this context:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ans=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{answer}},q,\mathcal{C}_{final})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ans=\text{LLM}(p_{\text{answer}},q,\mathcal{C}_{final}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Through this mechanism, ActMem transforms the agent from a passive retriever to an active reasoner, ensuring the utility of memory for decision-making.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The ActMemEval Benchmark</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section,
we introduce the pipeline to construct our ActMemEval and the evaluation results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.00026v1/dataset.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/174;" width="476" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Pipeline to construct our ActMemEval dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset Construction</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the reasoning capabilities of agents in long-term scenarios, we construct ActMemEval, a high-quality dataset featuring complex logical constraints and diverse linguistic styles.
Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 The ActMemEval Benchmark ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the construction pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Reasoning topology synthesis.</span>
Unlike previous datasets that generate dialogues directly, we first synthesize the underlying logic to ensure causal consistency. We employ an advanced LLM as the reasoning topology synthesizer. It generates a directed acyclic graph consisting of:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">History events: A sequence of background events (e.g., “User adopted a puppy”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning triggers: Intermediate states derived from history (e.g., “Puppy is teething”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current event: A user intent that conflicts with or relies on the history (e.g., “User wants to buy Sago Palms”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This structure explicitly defines the causal transitions and constraint activations required to solve the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Logical consistency validation.</span>
To reduce “hallucinated logic” or unsolvable queries, the generated topologies are passed to a logical consistency validator.
This module checks whether the reasoning chain from history to the current event is theoretically sound and unambiguous. Topologies containing broken links, weak correlations, or multiple plausible conflicting answers are filtered out, ensuring that every sample in our dataset has a logical ground truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Stylized dialogue generation.</span>
We convert the validated topologies into natural‑language dialogues using a dialogue editor.
To reflect real‑world diversity, we define a set of user linguistic styles along four dimensions: formality, verbosity, attitude, and urgency.
The logical nodes are then instantiated as anchor sessions, i.e., key dialogue turns containing essential evidence,
while following a sampled linguistic profile.
This design forces the agent to rely on semantic understanding rather than simple pattern matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Noise injection and stream assembly.</span>
In real-world interactions, key information is often sparse and hidden within casual chitchat. To reflect this, we build a noise session pool containing generic daily conversations (e.g., greetings, weather talk) and insert these sessions between the anchor sessions to form a full long‑context dialogue stream. This design tests the agent’s ability to filter out irrelevant content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5: Manual verification.</span>
Finally, to ensure the reliability of the benchmark, we employ a human‑in‑the‑loop verification process. Annotators examine the final dialogue streams to confirm that (1) logical constraints are preserved, (2) the answer is inferable from the given sessions, and (3) the text is natural and fluent. Among the 300 generated samples, only 246 that pass this inspection are retained in the final dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Dataset Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate our dataset,
we compute the semantic similarity (using cosine similarity of embeddings) between the ground-truth answers and the raw dialogue history.
We compare our ActMemEval with the retrieval‑based benchmark LongMemEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2 Dataset Evaluation ‣ 4 The ActMemEval Benchmark ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,
ActMemEval (red) exhibits substantially lower similarity, indicating that answers cannot be directly retrieved and instead require reasoning.
In contrast, LongMemEval (blue) shows high similarity, suggesting that it mainly evaluates recall ability.
Specifically, LongMemEval reaches an average similarity of 0.347, implying that many answers share considerable lexical or semantic overlap with the context.
ActMemEval yields a much lower average similarity of 0.232, a gap of more than 0.1, which confirms our answers are semantically distant from the raw history and must be inferred rather than extracted.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.00026v1/sim_longmemeval.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/194;" width="429" height="194"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Distribution of semantic similarity scores between the ground-truth answers and their corresponding context history.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the performance of ActMem,
we benchmark it against five representative memory frameworks:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NaiveRAG:</span> The standard baseline that retrieves the top-<math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
relevant dialogue snippets based on vector similarity and concatenates them into the context window.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mem0</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chhikara et al. (2025)</a></cite>: An advanced memory framework for personalized AI agents, focusing on efficient memory storage and graph-like organization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A-Mem</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite>: An agentic memory framework that emphasizes associative retrieval mechanisms to handle long-term interactions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MemoryOS</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al. (2025)</a></cite>: A system-level memory management framework that provides operating system-like interfaces for agents to read and write memories.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LightMem</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2025)</a></cite>: A efficient and SOTA memory framework utilizing hierarchical summaries to reduce token consumption while maintaining context.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ two key metrics to assess both the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">precision of recall</span> and the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">effectiveness of reasoning</span>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval accuracy:</span>
This evaluates whether the agent can successfully recall the specific memory facts required to resolve the query.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, retrieval accuracy is calculated as the recall rate of ground-truth evidence facts in our dataset.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question-answering accuracy:</span>
This metric measures the final utility of the memory.
It assesses whether the generated response correctly addresses the user intent while adhering to historical constraints.
Given the open-ended nature of generation, we utilize an LLM-as-a-Judge method (using DeepSeek-V3) to score the correctness of the response against the ground truth.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:107.8pt;vertical-align:-52.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-81.6pt,25.5pt) scale(0.67894399542028,0.67894399542028) ;">
