</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Efficiency Comparison</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.5 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents a multi-dimensional efficiency comparison versus QA accuracy.
As shown in Subfigure (a),
heavy frameworks like A-mem and MemoryOS incur prohibitive latency, yet fail to yield competitive accuracy.
In contrast, ActMem maintains high efficiency. Although it requires marginally more time than the summarization-based LightMem to construct causal graphs, it remains significantly faster than other graph-based baselines.
The second and third subfigures highlight the cost-effectiveness.
For the number of input tokens, ActMem consumes a moderate amount to extracting fine-grained causal events.
Regarding the number of output tokens,
ActMem achieves an optimal balance between token consumption and performance. We excluded MemoryOS from this comparison due to the difficulty in accessing its internally extracted memories for token quantification.
This indicates that our extraction and reasoning processes are concise and precise, avoiding wasteful hallucination or verbosity.
In summary, ActMem achieves an effective balance between reasoning performance and computational cost.
We can follow LightMem and apply compression techniques to reduce input tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.7 </span>Analysis of Semantic Similarity Thresholds</h3>

<div id="S5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the impact of the semantic similarity threshold on QA accuracy and graph density metrics.
As presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.5 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
we observe a clear correlation between graph connectivity and QA accuracy.
A lower threshold (e.g., 0.3) yields the optimal QA accuracy of 76.52%.
This improvement stems from the richer retrieved context: a relaxed threshold preserves more semantic edges, leading to a substantially higher average node degree (20.48) and more retrieved KG facts (5.21). The resulting dense connectivity provides the agent with broader semantically relevant background information, reducing the risk of missing critical clues.
In contrast, a stricter threshold (e.g., 0.7) produces a much sparser graph, with the average degree dropping to 2.34. Such aggressive pruning removes potential semantic associations, resulting in insufficient context retrieval (only 0.29 facts on average) and a corresponding decline in QA accuracy. These results highlight the trade‑off between graph sparsity and information recall.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.00026v1/longmemeval.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/84;" width="286" height="84"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Performance comparison on LongMemEval.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.8 </span>Results on Conventional Datasets</h3>

<div id="S5.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further verify the generalization capability and robustness of our ActMem,
we conduct additional evaluations on the established benchmark, LongMemEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
To ensure a fair comparison, we align our experimental settings with the SOTA method, LightMem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
We utilize GPT-4o-mini as the backbone LLM for both the memory reasoning process and the answer judgment.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.7 Analysis of Semantic Similarity Thresholds ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>,
ActMem achieves a remarkable QA accuracy of 75.60% on LongMemEval.
It outperforms LightMem by a significant margin of 6.96%.
This result demonstrates the great generalization capability of ActMem.
We attribute this success to our structured causal graph:
by organizing disjointed facts into coherent event clusters,
ActMem effectively reduces the noise and redundancy often present in flat vector retrieval, thereby enhancing the accuracy of answering factual queries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.9 </span>Case Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.5 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ActMem: Bridging the Gap Between Memory Retrieval and Reasoningin LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> presents a case study from our dataset.
The user asks for a location to buy “Sago Palms.”
Although the query appears simple, it contains a critical implicit constraint in the dialogue history: the user owns a puppy that is “teething” and “chewing on everything.”
As Sago Palms are toxic to dogs,
an intelligent agent should detect this risk and issue a warning.
LightMem fails to retrieve the relevant evidence about the puppy, instead surfacing irrelevant noise such as “high‑end hotels” and “cooking meals.”
It treats the query as a straightforward information‑seeking task and directly recommends where to buy the plants.
In contrast, ActMem successfully bridges this reasoning gap.
Through the counterfactual reasoning, the agent first infers the potential consequence of the user’s intent (i.e., that Sago Palms are toxic).
This knowledge becomes a semantic anchor that guides retrieval of the specific memory that the user’s puppy is chewing on everything.
By integrating this retrieved history with world knowledge,
ActMem identifies the conflict and produces a safety‑aware response, advising against the purchase.
This case highlights our ability to transform memory from passive storage into an active reasoning engine for robust decision‑making.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we identify a fundamental limitation in current LLM agent memory frameworks:
the disconnect between memory retrieval and reasoning.
We argue that high retrieval accuracy alone is insufficient for intelligent decision‑making, as agents must not only recall past information but also understand its implications for the present.
To address this issue, we propose ActMem, a hierarchical framework that transforms passive dialogue history into an active memory KG with both causal and semantic edges between facts.
ActMem enables agents to infer implicit constraints and resolve logical conflicts.
We further introduce a benchmark ActMemEval to evaluate the utility of memory for action.
Extensive experiments on both our dataset and standard benchmarks show that ActMem substantially outperforms baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In future work, we plan to explore lightweight LLMs to improve the efficiency of KG construction without compromising reasoning quality.
Second, we plan to extend ActMem to multimodal scenarios.
Finally, we will explore fine-tuning strategies to enhance the causal reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
